    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d200">

                <pb facs="0267" n="167" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg167"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/24174</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Summerlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-26"><hi rendition="#italic">May 26,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received June 5.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3166</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that with the
                    exception of minor cases of the “commandeering” of animals by revolutionary
                    troops, there have not been many causes for complaints by foreigners since the
                    revolutionary movement began. The conditions in Chiapas appear to be much more
                    healthy than formerly, but the sections or regions dominated by the Villistas
                    remain unchanged. If an open break between Obregón and Villa has occurred, as
                    has been reported, it will soon be apparent to what degree the new régime is
                    desirous of ridding the country of bandits. The press reports that the
                    Government of Chihuahua has already offered a reward of one hundred thousand
                    pesos for Villa, dead or alive.</p>
                <pb facs="0268" n="168" xml:id="pg168"/>
                <p>It appears to one in Mexico City that the more or less correct deportment of the
                    revolutionary forces throughout the country has been due to the desire of the
                    new régime to obtain at least <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> recognition
                    from the United States as soon as possible. (Such recognition from other
                    countries would follow). The statements of General Obregón as to the protection
                    of foreigners and their interests in Mexico and as to the necessity of harmony
                    and understanding between Mexico and the United States point to the same
                    desire.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George T.
                    Summerlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
